bug_descriptions |bug_id, line_num, content|
    seed_lowering_reports_only_first_type_error_2026-11-26, 0, "simple compile/run stop at the first HIR lowering error, so later type errors in the same import closure are never reported. Repro: a file with fn f() -> i64: return 1.5, then fn h() -> text: d.get(\"k\") (Dict<text, text>), then main: compile prints only the E1003 for f, never h. Impact: the 2026-11-26 E1003 sweep (23,072 files) saw 11,832 files exit 1 for other reasons first; an E1003 behind any earlier lowering error stays hidden until that one is fixed, so a 0-hit sweep is a lower bound. Expected: lowering collects type errors per function and reports all of them (or at least every E1003) before failing."

bugs_active |id, severity, status, title, file, line, reproducible_by, created_at, updated_at, valid|
    seed_lowering_reports_only_first_type_error_2026-11-26, P3, closed, "stale: FIXED by p3sweep2-20261127 (second HIR pass now collects E1003-class verdicts across all functions and prints them, first error still primary; spec RED-to-GREEN; 494 hir::lower cargo tests pass; pure-Simple lane verified already complete) 2026-11-27. Was: Seed lowering stops at the first type error", src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/hir/lower/module_lowering/function.rs, 1, simple compile <file with two E1003 functions> -o /dev/null, 2026-11-26, 2026-11-27, true

bug_investigation_logs |bug_id, line_num, content|
    seed_lowering_reports_only_first_type_error_2026-11-26, 0, "2026-11-26 filed from the optional_return_into_scalar_fn_accepted_2026-11-26 full-tree sweep; reproduced on the seed with the E1003 rule (fix6)."
    seed_lowering_reports_only_first_type_error_2026-11-26, 0, "2026-11-27 p3sweep2-20261127: reproduced (two independent E1003 functions: only f's error printed, h's never). Root cause: the second module pass in src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/hir/lower/module_lowering/module_pass.rs lowered each body with the ? operator, so the first LowerError unwound the whole pass and later functions were never lowered. Fix: the second pass collects only E1003-class verdicts (LowerError::Unsupported messages tagged error[E1003]) across every lower_function call site (free fns, class/struct/enum methods, mixins, actors, impl blocks, trait defaults) via lower_function_collecting_type_errors; all other errors still abort immediately; after the loop the pass fails with the FIRST collected error (unchanged primary diagnostic + exit code) having printed the rest; non-E1003 aborts flush everything collected first. function.rs: lower_function split into wrapper + lower_function_body; error paths now restore current_class_type/current_function_*/the flatten overlay and close the lifetime scope (required because the collector continues; provably identical for pre-existing callers). Verification on the scratch build: two_errs prints BOTH E1003 lines with f primary, exit 1; single-error output byte-identical to unpatched; clean file still compiles; mixed E1003+OptionalCast probe flushes the E1003 with the cast error primary (strictly more complete). Spec test/01_unit/compiler/seed_lowering_reports_all_type_errors_spec.spl RED on unpatched fresh-seed (fails exactly on the second_bad assertion), GREEN on the scratch build; cargo test --release -p simple-compiler --lib hir::lower 494 passed 0 failed. Pure-Simple lane: NO defect - its errors collector reports errors from all functions (in-process probe: both unknown types reported, 4 entries over two passes), and E1003 return-mismatches are deliberately admitted leniently in the self-hosted lane. Residuals: per-function multi-error collection (multiple E1003s inside ONE body) needs stmt_lowering.rs/operators.rs - out of scope, doc acceptance at least every E1003 across functions is met; deployed-seed verification runs with the next seed rebuild (recipe in the spec header, SIMPLE_DIAGNOSTIC_SEED_BIN override). close_evidence reproducer=test/01_unit/compiler/seed_lowering_reports_all_type_errors_spec.spl prevention=test/01_unit/compiler/95.interp/interpreter/division_by_zero_span_diagnostic_spec.spl"

